1
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:13,000
This is a hospital management system
written in nothing but Java.

2
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:34,600
Forty-five source files.
Nine and a half thousand lines.

3
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:38,400
No Spring. No Maven. No Gradle.

4
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:44,000
Not one third-party library — and three
hundred and twenty-five tests that all pass.

5
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,200
Watch the order these four lines appear in.

6
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:06,400
Constants initialises first — a static block,
running before a single object exists.

7
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,500
AppConfig is an eager singleton. Built during
class initialisation, and thread-safe for free,

8
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:17,800
because the JVM runs that exactly once
under its own lock.

9
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:22,000
IdGenerator is lazy. It waits until
something actually asks for it.

10
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:28,800
And MedicalEntity — the root of the entire
class hierarchy — loads last.

11
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:35,800
Not because it matters least. Because the JVM
does not load a class until it is first used.

12
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:40,000
That ordering isn't documentation.
It's the class loader, caught in the act.

13
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:47,000
The architecture is a directed acyclic graph,
and it is enforced by the compiler.

14
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:53,500
Contracts at the bottom, depending on nothing.
The domain above them. Services orchestrating it.

15
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:57,500
And the console — deliberately thin — on top.

16
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,500
Four people built this, and ownership runs
by package, not by file.

17
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:23,000
Two engineers editing different methods in one file
still collide. Different packages essentially never do.

18
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,500
And one rule holds the whole thing up. Main reads
input, calls a service, prints the result.

19
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:35,000
It validates nothing. It stores nothing.
It prices nothing.

20
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:40,000
Without that discipline, console applications
reliably become god objects.

21
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
One bill. Three design patterns,
each doing real work.

22
00:02:54,500 --> 00:03:01,500
Factory chose the subclass — and its switch is
exhaustive, so adding a bill type without

23
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:05,500
handling it is a compile error,
not a silently unpriced bill.

24
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:13,000
Strategy chose the pricing policy. Nobody selected
"senior citizen" — Ravi is sixty-seven, and the

25
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:16,500
policy was derived from his own record.

26
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:23,500
Template Method fixed the order. generateBill is
final. The sequence is a business invariant,

27
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:26,800
so no subclass can discount after tax.

28
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:31,500
Fifteen hundred. Less ten percent.
Then eighteen percent tax on what remains.

29
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:35,000
The discount lands before the tax — because
the template will not permit anything else.

30
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,500
Type symptoms in plain English,
and it routes you.

31
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:47,500
No machine learning. No network call. Keyword rules
declared on the enum itself, so the mapping lives

32
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:50,800
next to the speciality it describes.

33
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:56,500
And it shows its work — which keywords matched,
the confidence, and why each doctor ranked where they did.

34
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:04,000
Experience is capped at twenty years, so a long-serving
generalist can never outrank a well-matched specialist.

35
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:13,000
SonarQube's first pass came back with thirteen bugs
and a reliability rating of E. The worst there is.

36
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:21,500
A leaked thread pool. Uptime measured off a wall clock
that could run backwards. Duration maths that would

37
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:25,000
put reminders an hour out, twice a year.

38
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
All thirteen are fixed. Zero bugs.
Zero vulnerabilities. Rating A.

39
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:34,000
And where it isn't perfect,
the documentation says so.

40
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
MediTrack. Forty-five files, zero dependencies,
three hundred and twenty-five tests.

41
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:51,500
Open source, under MIT.

42
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:56,500
Read it. Run it. Or contribute to it.
